    "narrative": "As we navigate this late-season stretch, our offensive output today relies heavily on the limited volume available due to league-wide scheduling gaps. **Liam Hicks** stands as our premier play with a score of 51.09, elevated by his prime spot in the three-hole and a favorable matchup against Armstrong (xERA 4.35). The dynamic platoon advantage remains the primary catalyst for **Liam Hicks**, as his historical consistency against right-handed pitching provides the highest floor on an otherwise quiet day. While we maintain significant talent on the bench\u2014including heavy hitters like **Kyle Tucker**, **Gunnar Henderson**, and **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.**\u2014the lack of active games for those stars leaves us reliant on the fringes of our roster to manufacture production.\n\n**Brayan Rocchio** rounds out our active starters with a marginal score of 40.89, narrowly clearing our threshold despite a sub-optimal ninth-spot placement in the lineup. The decision to roster **Brayan Rocchio** over potential depth options was driven by his relative stability against Peralta, F (xERA 4.32), though his ceiling is severely capped by the batting order anchor. We are keeping a close watch on the status of **Luis Robert Jr.**, who remains sidelined on the IL, as his absence continues to weigh on our speed category potential. With **Jackson Merrill**, **Andy Pages**, **Jordan Westburg**, **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.**, **Drake Baldwin**, **Jacob Wilson**, **Heliot Ramos**, **Nasim Nunez**, **Sam Antonacci**, **Miguel Vargas**, **Josh Jung**, **Coby Mayo**, **Dominic Smith**, **Curtis Mead**, **Wade Meckler**, **Luis Lara**, **Henry Bolte**, and **Franklin Arias** all unavailable for points today, we must prioritize efficiency over raw volume. We will look to **Liam Hicks** to provide the necessary runs and RBI while hoping **Brayan Rocchio** can avoid a sub-floor performance against an arm that, while hittable, has historically stifled our middle-infield production.",
